---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="How Many Fence Pickets Do I Need for 100 Feet? | CalcForDIY"
  description="Learn how to calculate fence pickets for a 100-foot fence, accounting for picket widths (3.5 to 5.5 in), gap spacing, post deductions, gate openings, and waste factors."
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline>
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Article",
    "headline": "How Many Fence Pickets Do I Need for 100 Feet?",
    "description": "Comprehensive guide on calculating fence pickets, gap spacing, post deductions, and material takeoffs for a 100-foot fence.",
    "author": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "CalcForDIY Engineering" },
    "publisher": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "CalcForDIY", "url": "https://calcfordiy.com" }
  }
  </script>

  <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline>
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "How many 6-inch pickets do I need for a 100 ft fence?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Standard 6-inch pickets actually measure 5.5 inches wide. For a 100-foot privacy fence with zero gaps, you need 218 pickets. If you leave a 1/2-inch expansion gap, you need 200 pickets before accounting for post deductions and waste."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Should I leave a gap between fence pickets?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Yes. Wood expands and contracts with humidity changes. Pressure-treated wood is often wet when installed and will shrink, creating a 1/8 to 1/4 inch gap over time. Dry cedar pickets should be installed with a 1/8 to 1/4 inch gap to prevent warping when wet."
        }
      }
    ]
  }
  </script>

  <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-sm text-gray-500 mb-4 print:hidden">
    <a href="/" class="hover:text-blue-600">Home</a>
    <span>/</span>
    <a href="/guides" class="hover:text-blue-600">Guides</a>
    <span>/</span>
    <span>Fence Pickets for 100 Foot Fence</span>
  </div>

  <article class="max-w-3xl">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6">How Many Fence Pickets Do I Need for 100 Feet?</h1>
    
    <div class="prose prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 space-y-5">
      <div class="p-5 bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-blue-950 mt-0 mb-2">⚡ Quick Answer Summary:</h2>
        <p class="text-blue-900 text-base leading-relaxed mb-0">
          For a <strong>100-foot fence</strong> using standard <strong>5.5-inch wide pickets</strong> (sold as 6-inch nominal width):
          <br />• <strong>Solid Privacy (0" Gap):</strong> 218 pickets
          <br />• <strong>Standard Expansion Gap (1/2" Gap):</strong> 200 pickets
          <br />• <strong>Traditional Spaced Picket (2" Gap):</strong> 160 pickets
          <br /><em>Accounting for 13 4x4 posts, a 4-foot gate, and a 10% waste buffer, order <strong>205 pickets</strong>.</em>
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="text-lg">
        Calculating the exact number of pickets for a wood fence requires more than dividing total length by picket width. Real lumber dimensions, gap spacing, post displacement, gate openings, and wood splitting during installation all change your final count.
      </p>

      <!-- Calculator CTA -->
      <div class="my-8 p-6 bg-gradient-to-br from-blue-50 to-indigo-100 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl shadow-sm">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-950 mb-2 mt-0">Skip the Manual Math: Use Our Free Fence Calculator</h2>
        <p class="text-blue-900 mb-4">Enter your fence length, picket width, post spacing, and gate counts to calculate pickets, 2x4 rails, 4x4 posts, and concrete bags instantly.</p>
        <a href="/calculators/fence-pickets" class="inline-block bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white font-semibold py-3 px-6 rounded-lg transition-colors">
          Go to the Fence Pickets Calculator →
        </a>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">1. Nominal vs. Actual Picket Widths</h2>
      <p>
        Just like a 2x4 board actually measures 1.5" &times; 3.5", fence pickets are sold by their <strong>nominal dimensions</strong>:
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-2">
        <li><strong>"4-Inch" Picket:</strong> Actual width is <strong>3.5 inches</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>"6-Inch" Picket:</strong> Actual width is <strong>5.5 inches</strong> (most common cedar and pressure-treated pine picket).</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">2. Picket Count Reference Table for a 100-Foot Fence</h2>
      <p>
        Below is the exact picket count needed for a 100-foot run (1,200 inches) across different picket widths and gap spacings:
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
        <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse border border-gray-200 text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-gray-100 border-b border-gray-200">
              <th class="p-3 font-semibold text-gray-900">Picket Size (Actual Width)</th>
              <th class="p-3 font-semibold text-gray-900">Gap Spacing</th>
              <th class="p-3 font-semibold text-gray-900">Effective Width</th>
              <th class="p-3 font-semibold text-gray-900">Base Picket Count (100 Ft)</th>
              <th class="p-3 font-semibold text-gray-900">With 10% Waste Buffer</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200">
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">5.5" (6" Nominal)</td>
              <td class="p-3">0" (Solid Privacy)</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">5.5"</td>
              <td class="p-3">219 pickets</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-bold text-blue-700">241 pickets</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-blue-50">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">5.5" (6" Nominal)</td>
              <td class="p-3">1/2" (Standard)</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">6.0"</td>
              <td class="p-3">200 pickets</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-bold text-blue-700">220 pickets</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">5.5" (6" Nominal)</td>
              <td class="p-3">2.0" (Spaced Picket)</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">7.5"</td>
              <td class="p-3">160 pickets</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-bold text-blue-700">176 pickets</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">3.5" (4" Nominal)</td>
              <td class="p-3">0" (Solid Privacy)</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">3.5"</td>
              <td class="p-3">343 pickets</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-bold text-blue-700">377 pickets</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">3.5" (4" Nominal)</td>
              <td class="p-3">1/2" (Standard)</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono">4.0"</td>
              <td class="p-3">300 pickets</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-bold text-blue-700">330 pickets</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">3. Step-by-Step Calculation Example with Deductions</h2>
      <p>
        Let's calculate a real-world 100-foot backyard fence line that includes <strong>4x4 posts spaced 8 feet on-center</strong> and one <strong>4-foot (48") walk-through gate</strong>:
      </p>

      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-5 space-y-3 font-mono text-sm">
        <p><strong>Step 1: Total Length in Inches</strong> = 100 ft &times; 12 = 1,200 inches</p>
        <p><strong>Step 2: Number of 4x4 Posts</strong> = (100 ft &divide; 8 ft) + 1 = 13 posts</p>
        <p><strong>Step 3: Deduct Gate Opening</strong> = 1,200" &minus; 48" = 1,152" remaining</p>
        <p><strong>Step 4: Deduct Exposed Post Widths</strong> (13 posts @ 3.5" = 45.5") = 1,106.5" net picket run</p>
        <p><strong>Step 5: Divide by Effective Width (5.5" + 0.5" gap = 6.0")</strong> = 1,106.5 &divide; 6.0 = 184.4 pickets</p>
        <p><strong>Step 6: Add Gate Pickets</strong> = 48" &divide; 6.0" = 8 pickets</p>
        <p><strong>Step 7: Total Net Pickets</strong> = 184.4 + 8 = 192.4 pickets</p>
        <p><strong>Step 8: Apply 10% Waste Factor</strong> = 192.4 &times; 1.10 = <strong>212 Pickets</strong></p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">4. Full Material Takeoff for a 100-Foot Fence</h2>
      <p>When buying materials for a 100-foot wood fence project, you also need:</p>

      <ul class="space-y-2">
        <li><strong>4x4 Pressure-Treated Posts (8 ft long):</strong> 13 posts</li>
        <li><strong>2x4 Backer Rails (16 ft long):</strong> 20 to 30 rails (3 rails per section for 6 ft high fences)</li>
        <li><strong>Concrete Bags (80 lb Quikrete):</strong> 26 bags (2 bags per post hole below frost line)</li>
        <li><strong>Fasteners:</strong> ~1,200 ring-shank exterior stainless or hot-dipped galvanized nails (6 nails per picket)</li>
      </ul>

      <!-- Bottom Calculator CTA -->
      <div class="mt-10 p-6 bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl text-center">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Ready to Calculate Your Exact Fence Takeoff?</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-600 text-sm mb-4">Use our free fence pickets calculator to get instant picket counts, 2x4 backer rails, 4x4 posts, and concrete bag estimates.</p>
        <a href="/calculators/fence-pickets" class="inline-block bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white font-semibold py-3 px-8 rounded-lg transition-colors">
          Open Fence Pickets Calculator →
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
